We can implement an iterative function to compute this:
// REQUIRES: n >= 0
// EFFECTS: Computes and returns n!.
int factorial(int n) {
int result = 1;
while (n > 0) {
result *= n;
--n;
}
return result;
}
On the other hand, we can observe that (𝑛−1)! = (𝑛−1) * · · · * 1, so that we can mathematically define factorial in
terms of itself:
𝑛! =
{︃
1
